<script>
  import Meta from '../components/Meta.brm';
  import Network from '../components/Network.brm';
  import Shipped from '../components/Shipped.brm';
  import Waterfall from '../components/Waterfall.brm';
  import { serverFunctions, streaming, typed } from '../home/snippets.ts';
  import * as counter from '../demos/Counter.brm?inspect';
  import * as todos from '../demos/Todos.brm?inspect';
  import Shop from '../demos/Shop.brm';
  import * as shop from '../demos/Shop.brm?inspect';

  const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
  const shopKb = (shop.report.sizes.reduce((sum, size) => sum + size.gzip, 0) / 1024).toFixed(2);
</script>

#head {
  <title>brumate: web apps that resume</title>
}

<Meta
  title="brumate: web apps that resume"
  description="A component language and compiler. Components render on the server, and the browser loads each handler only when it's needed. No hydration."
  path=""
/>
<main class="home">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <p class="eyebrow">A component language and compiler</p>
    <h1>Web apps that <span class="gradient-text">resume</span>, not hydrate.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Components render on the server. The browser keeps their state and loads each handler
      only when it's needed. Pages work at once, and nothing runs twice.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href=(`${base}docs/getting-started`)>Get started</a>
      <a class="button" href=(`${base}repl`)>Open the REPL</a>
      <a class="button" href=(`${base}docs`)>Read the docs</a>
    </div>
    <pre class="install"><span class="prompt">$</span> pnpm add brumate</pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>After the HTML arrives</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      A page that hydrates runs every component again in the browser before the first click works.
      A brumate page picks up where the server left off.
    </p>
    <Waterfall />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Try it on this page</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      This shop page is a brumate component, rendered when this page was built. The list on the right shows every script the page has loaded.
      Before you touch it, the page holds only the loader. Pick a color or add to the cart, and watch its code arrive.
      Turn on X-ray to see which parts ship code and which ship as HTML only.
    </p>
    <div class="playground">
      <div class="xray">
        <label class="xray-toggle">
          <input type="checkbox">
          <span>X-ray: what ships</span>
        </label>
        <div class="browser">
          <div class="browser-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>kettle.example/pour-over</em></div>
          <Shop />
        </div>
        <ul class="xray-legend">
          <li class="html">HTML only. No code ships for it.</li>
          <li class="handler">A handler. Its code loads when its event fires.</li>
          <li class="update">Updates when a value it reads changes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="xray-sum">
          The whole component is <strong>{shopKb} KB</strong> gzip, and none of it loads before the first click.{' '}
          {shop.report.staticMarkup.elements} of its elements ship as HTML only. The X-ray switch is CSS, with no script.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Network />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What the compiler ships</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      The compiler builds the graph of what reads what once, when it compiles. Each handler is its own module.
      Markup that cannot change ships as HTML only. These tables come from <code>brumate inspect</code>, run when this site was built.
    </p>
    <div class="showcase">
      <article class="showcase-item">
        <h3>Counter</h3>
        {counter.source}
        <Shipped report=(counter.report) />
      </article>
      <article class="showcase-item">
        <h3>Todos</h3>
        {todos.source}
        <Shipped report=(todos.report) />
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section rows">
    <article class="row">
      <div class="row-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Streaming</p>
        <h2>Send the page now, and the data when it is ready</h2>
        <p>
          A <code>#loading</code> block shows its <code>#else</code> body in the first response.
          The server keeps the connection open and streams the real body in place once its data loads.
          The browser needs no code for it.
        </p>
        <a href=(`${base}docs/async-data`)>Loading data</a>
      </div>
      <div class="row-media">
        <div class="code">{streaming}</div>
        <div class="browser" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="browser-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="browser-page stream-demo">
            <div class="stream-skeleton"><i></i><i></i></div>
            <div class="stream-loaded"><b>Ada Lovelace</b><span>Wrote the first program.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row">
      <div class="row-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Server functions</p>
        <h2>Call the server like a function</h2>
        <p>
          Mark an async function with <code>'use server'</code>. Its body and its imports, such as a database client,
          stay on the server. The browser gets a small function that sends the call. A form can post to one with no script at all.
        </p>
        <a href=(`${base}docs/server-functions`)>Server functions</a>
      </div>
      <div class="row-media">
        <div class="code">{serverFunctions}</div>
        <div class="wire" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="wire-end">Browser</span>
          <span class="wire-line"><span class="wire-packet">like(1)</span></span>
          <span class="wire-end">Server</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row">
      <div class="row-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Typed</p>
        <h2>Errors before the page breaks</h2>
        <p>
          <code>brumate-tsc</code> checks scripts, markup, props and events. It also checks that every value the browser reads can be sent to it.
        </p>
        <a href=(`${base}docs/typescript`)>TypeScript</a>
      </div>
      <div class="row-media">
        <div class="code">{typed}</div>
        <p class="type-error"><span>error</span> A function cannot be sent to the client. Keep it out of the signal or memo</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row">
      <div class="row-text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Routes</p>
        <h2>Files are routes</h2>
        <p>
          A <code>.brm</code> file under <code>src/routes</code> renders its URL. Layouts wrap the routes of their folder.
          Links load pages in place and keep the layouts, with their state.
        </p>
        <a href=(`${base}docs/routing`)>Routing</a>
      </div>
      <div class="row-media">
        <ul class="tree">
          <li><code>src/routes/</code></li>
          <li class="nest"><code>_layout.brm</code><span>wraps every page</span></li>
          <li class="nest"><code>index.brm</code><span>/</span></li>
          <li class="nest"><code>docs/</code></li>
          <li class="nest2"><code>[page].brm</code><span>/docs/routing</span></li>
          <li class="nest"><code>[...rest].brm</code><span>every other path</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>And the rest of an app</h2>
    <div class="features">
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Forms without scripts</h3>
        <p>A form posts to a server function before any script loads, and with scripts off. Once the loader runs, it posts without a page load.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Scoped styles</h3>
        <p>A <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block matches only its component. Values from the script go into the CSS and update with the state.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Shared logic</h3>
        <p>A <code>.brm.ts</code> module holds signals and memos that several components use, compiled like the script of a component.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Tests that resume</h3>
        <p><code>brumate/testing</code> renders on the server and resumes in the DOM of a test, with the code that ships.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Deploy anywhere</h3>
        <p>Serve pages from your own server, build for Node, Bun, Deno, Cloudflare or Vercel with Nitro, or prerender a site that has no server functions.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>An editor that knows .brm</h3>
        <p>The VS Code extension gives <code>.brm</code> files highlighting, types and the compiler's errors. Other editors use the same language server.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta">
    <h2>Start with one component</h2>
    <p>A <code>.brm</code> file, the Vite plugin and a server that streams pages.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href=(`${base}docs/getting-started`)>Get started</a>
      <a class="button" href=(`${base}repl`)>Open the REPL</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
